        "title": "Cure-all pills",
        "subtitle": "Myth or reality?",
        "text": "Look along the shelves of any health food shop or chemist's and you will find dozens of dietary supplements: vitamins, antioxidants and minerals, along with an apparently endless range of homeopathic remedies. There is always something new, some \"miracle ingredient\" that promises a longer, healthier, happier life. There are extracts of every fruit and vegetable you can think of (tomato, celery, carrot, papaya and cranberry), as well as green tea potions, red wine extract and vitamins A to K in every colour and form. All of these supplements claim to help us in the constant battle against ageing, cancer, heart disease and a whole range of other illnesses. However, recent research may show that it is all too good to be true.\n\nSo how real are these claims? Vitamin tablets have been around for ever, but antioxidants are the latest miracle cure. The question is: do they work? If the publicity is true, antioxidants work by neutralising the free radicals in our bodies and then getting rid of them. Free radicals are atoms or molecules with at least one unpaired electron, which makes them unstable and highly reactive. In animal tissue they are thought to speed up the progress of heart disease and age-related diseases such as dementia and cancer. Antioxidants in fresh fruit and vegetables have been shown to combine with free radicals and neutralise them, preventing the oxidation which may lead to disease.\n\n\"An apple a day keeps the doctor away\", as long as it is eaten together with four other portions of fresh fruit and vegetables! It is clear that people who eat more fruit and vegetables (the recommended daily amount is at least five portions) are less likely to become ill and live longer, healthier lives. Over the last 20 to 30 years, scientists have worked to isolate the active ingredients in fruit and vegetables that are thought to be the antioxidants, and they have carried out controlled tests on them. These tests showed that antioxidants do seem able to stop some of the chemical processes that cause disease. And so the multimillion-dollar industry of bottled antioxidants, sold to add to the diets of busy, stressed people, was born.\n\nDoubt was first cast on antioxidants during a major clinical trial in the US. A very common antioxidant, beta-carotene, also known as pro-vitamin A (found in yellow and green vegetables, milk and fish), was being tested to see whether it worked against lung cancer in high-risk people. To the surprise and concern of the scientists running the trial, the people taking the supplement, which was meant to reduce their risk of cancer, had a much higher risk of developing lung cancer. This shocking discovery led to the trial being stopped halfway through.\n\nSince this experiment in the mid-1990s, other studies have also suggested that antioxidants and vitamin supplements are of doubtful health value at best, and may possibly be harmful. It seems that even common vitamin supplements such as vitamin C may, in large doses, actually increase the risk of heart disease or cancer.\n\nAs a result of these worrying studies, the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and its European equivalent, the European Medicines Agency (EMEA), have decided to ban the production and sale of a number of the higher-dose supplements on the market.\n\nThe picture is becoming more and more complicated, because recent research has also questioned some earlier beliefs about free radicals. Some scientists believe it is possible that free radicals actually play a role in preventing disease. Professor Jennifer Horton at the University of West Wyoming is one of a number of scientists who have found that, in small amounts, free radicals may help to stimulate the antioxidant systems in our bodies. It is also becoming clear that free radicals may play a fundamental role in the fight against disease: recent research in the UK suggests that they kill harmful bacteria and even cancer cells.\n\nClearly, then, adding bottled supplements to your diet is a practice to approach warily, and it is essential to keep up to date with new research. Ironically, the health-conscious people who already eat large amounts of fresh fruit and vegetables, who do not eat junk food and who get plenty of regular exercise, and so have less need for dietary supplements, tend to be the ones who are most likely to use them."
